name 'pl-aif1-js-conditionals-and-boolean'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Conditional Statements and Boolean Expressions</h1>

<p>The diamond shape from the last level, the one asking a Yes/No question, is the flowchart version of something you'll see constantly in code: a conditional statement.</p>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/7f03068dffe3b229af744c8309872947-image-1786465958176.png" alt="An illustration of a fork in the road with two signs, one reading TRUE and one reading FALSE, representing a program choosing between two paths based on a condition." style="width: 35%; max-width: 180px; border-radius: 10px; flex-shrink: 0;">
  <div style="flex: 1;">
    <div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
      <p>📖 <b>Conditional Statement</b> is a piece of code that runs different actions depending on whether something is true or false.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
      <p>📖 <b>Boolean Expression</b> is a statement that the computer evaluates as either true or false.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The Boolean expression is the question itself, the thing being checked. The conditional statement is what happens as a result, one path if the answer is true, a different path if it's false.</p>

<h2>Seeing It in Code</h2>

<p>Here's a conditional statement checking a temperature value from a slider:</p>
<div style="background-color:#171717; color:#F5F5F5; border-radius:10px; padding:18px 22px; font-family:'Courier New', Courier, monospace; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; overflow-x:auto; white-space:nowrap; box-shadow:0 3px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);">
  <span style="color:#D987E8;">if</span> (temperature &gt; <span style="color:#F6AD72;">70</span>) {<br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:#B680F2;">message</span>.textContent = <span style="color:#F6AD72;">"It's warm outside!"</span>;<br>
  } <span style="color:#D987E8;">else</span> {<br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:#B680F2;">message</span>.textContent = <span style="color:#F6AD72;">"It's cool outside!"</span>;<br>
  }
</div>
<br>
<p><code style="font-size: 14px;">temperature &gt; 70</code> is the Boolean expression, it can only ever evaluate to true or false. The <code  style="font-size: 14px;">if</code> and <code  style="font-size: 14px;">else</code> blocks are the conditional statement, the two different actions the program takes depending on which way that expression comes out.</p>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<p><strong>💡 Teacher tip:</strong> when students explore an app like this, they'll typically change the message text, a color, or the condition itself, something like changing 70 to 80. All three are valid ways to interact with a conditional, and noticing which one a student changed tells you something about what they understood: changing the message shows they found the conditional, changing the number shows they understood what it's actually checking.</p>
</div>

<p>Next, you'll see this exact temperature example broken down piece by piece, connecting each part of the flowchart to the line of code it became.</p>


MARKDOWN
